Your catch block probably assumes err.message exists. JavaScript doesn't guarantee it.

`throw` accepts any expression: a string, a number, an object. MDN says you should always throw Error objects, but that's advice, not a rule. A library, a promise rejection or old code can still hand you something else.

That's why TypeScript's useUnknownInCatchVariables flag types the catch variable as unknown instead of any:

try { await save(); }
catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof Error) log(err.message);
  else log(String(err));
}

With any, err.message compiles and then fails on a thrown string. With unknown, the compiler makes you check first.

My rule of thumb: keep the flag on, narrow once at the boundary, and always throw real Error objects in your own code.

Do you handle non-Error throws in one helper, or inline in every catch?
